{"id":"h03","type":"text","x":-1000,"y":-850,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**03 Vertical structure**\nGas law + weight of the air → pressure, scale height, lapse rate."},
{"id":"h04","type":"text","x":-590,"y":-850,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**04 Greenhouse & RCE**\nWhere infrared escapes + convection set surface temperature."},
{"id":"h01","type":"text","x":-180,"y":-850,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**01 Energy budget & CERES**\nSun in = infrared out globally, but not locally."},
{"id":"h02","type":"text","x":230,"y":-850,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**02 Feedbacks & Daisyworld**\nA response that amplifies or damps its own cause."},
{"id":"h14","type":"text","x":640,"y":-850,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**14 Carbon**\nCO₂ → surface → buffer → deep ocean (≈1000 yr)."},
{"id":"h09","type":"text","x":-1000,"y":-300,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**09 Heat capacity & inertia**\nDeep mixed layer turns weather noise into slow SST swings."},
{"id":"h07","type":"text","x":-590,"y":-300,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**07 Rotation → winds**\nCoriolis → geostrophy → thermal wind → trades & jets."},
{"id":"h10","type":"text","x":230,"y":-300,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**10 Wind → Ekman → gyres**\nWind stress → Ekman → pumping → gyres → boundary currents."},
{"id":"h11","type":"text","x":640,"y":-300,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**11 Salt, density, water masses**\nE−P−R + cooling decide what sinks, and where."},
{"id":"h08","type":"text","x":-1000,"y":250,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**08 Monsoon**\nSeasonal ITCZ shift + land–sea heat contrast."},
{"id":"h06","type":"text","x":-590,"y":250,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**06 Hadley cell**\nAngular-momentum conservation shapes the tropical overturning."},
{"id":"h05","type":"text","x":-180,"y":250,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**05 Poleward heat transport**\nTropical surplus, polar deficit: air and ocean carry the heat."},
{"id":"h13","type":"text","x":230,"y":250,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**13 ENSO**\nBjerknes feedback grows it; Kelvin/Rossby waves turn it around."},
{"id":"h12","type":"text","x":640,"y":250,"width":300,"height":200,"text":"**12 Overturning & Stommel switch**\nSink in the north, return via the Southern Ocean; can switch off."},
{"id":"g10c","type":"text","text":"**Chain · momentum ledger** — wind stress (trades ↔ westerlies) ⟶ Ekman transport **90° right** ⟶ diverges ⇒ **up** (coasts, equator, Southern Ocean) · converges ⇒ **down** and builds a **1 m hill** ⟶ geostrophic flow **around** the hill (gyres, fast on the **west**) ⟶ momentum leaves at coasts and the sea floor (ACC).\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":-930,"width":2020,"height":140},
{"id":"g10s1","type":"text","text":"**1 · Wind stress on the ocean**\nTrades push the water west, westerlies east; τ ≈ 0.1 N m⁻², strongest over the Southern Ocean. The ocean is *ponderous* (ρ ×850 air): 0.1 N m⁻² on a 50 m layer would reach 0.1 m s⁻¹ in ≈ 14 h if nothing opposed it. [[Wind Stress]]\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"2"},
{"id":"g10s2","type":"text","text":"**2 · Ekman transport: 90° to the right**\nAdd up all layers: the internal drags cancel in pairs, only τ(top) − τ(bottom ≈ 0) is left ⇒ M = τ/ρf ≈ 1 m² s⁻¹, **90° right** of the wind (N), left (S). Needs no eddy viscosity. [[Ekman Transport]]\n💡 A deck of cards: each hands its push down; only the top and the bottom of the deck count.\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g10m1","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• **f = 2Ω sin φ**: M ∝ 1/f, so it grows toward the equator and breaks down within ≈ 5°\n• the **spiral**: surface current 45° right, but the *total* is still 90° ⇒ Nansen's ice (20–40°) marks only the top\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g10s3","type":"text","text":"**3 · Ekman pumping**\nThe wind changes from place to place, so the transport does too, and mass conservation forces vertical motion: w = curl(τ/ρf) ≈ 6.5×10⁻⁷ m s⁻¹ ≈ **20 m yr⁻¹**. More leaves than arrives ⇒ water **rises**; more arrives ⇒ it **sinks**. [[Ekman Pumping]]\n[Joy]","x":2410,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g10m2","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• the **curl** of the wind (its change with place), not its size\n• 2011 map: subtropical gyres pushed **down** 25–35 m yr⁻¹; subpolar N Pacific (≈ 15) and Southern Ocean 50–62°S (≈ 25) pulled **up**\n• blank within 5° of the equator and next to coasts\n[Joy]","x":2410,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g10s4","type":"text","text":"**4 · Upwelling: cold water arrives (the diverging case)**\n**Coast:** wind along the coast (NH: land on its left; SH: on its right) ⇒ transport offshore ⇒ deep water rises (California, Peru, Somalia). **Equator:** f changes sign ⇒ transport leaves on both sides ⇒ **cold tongue**. Closes the heat puzzle of [[CL23 The Ocean's Ledger II - Heat Capacity and Climate Inertia]]. [[Upwelling]]\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g10m3","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• **trade-wind strength**: weaker trades (El Niño) ⇒ weaker upwelling ⇒ warmer tongue → cascade 13 (ENSO)\n• the **wall** at a coast and f → 0 at the equator, which the pumping map leaves blank\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g10s5","type":"text","text":"**5 · Convergence: the 1 m hill**\nThe mirror case. Trades move water poleward, westerlies equatorward; they meet near 30°, water piles up and is pushed down: a **hill ≈ 1 m high, thousands of km across** (N Pacific 0.93, N Atlantic 0.94, S Pacific 1.05 m above the subpolar ocean). [[Sea-Surface Height]] [[Subtropical Gyre]]\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g10s6","type":"text","text":"**6 · Geostrophy: the hill stays up**\nThe push is downhill, but Coriolis turns the water right until it cancels the push, so it runs **along the height contours, high on its right** (N): nothing runs downhill. u = (g/f) × slope: 1 m over 1,500 km ≈ **6.5 cm s⁻¹**, 1 m over 100 km ≈ **1 m s⁻¹**. [[Geostrophic Balance]]\n💡 Suhas's wind round a high, in water.\n[Joy] [S-post]","x":2410,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g10m4","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• **scale and time**: only for flows larger than ≈ 50 km and longer than a few days (Rossby number ≈ 10⁻³ for 0.1 m s⁻¹ over 1,000 km)\n• **slope**: steeper ⇒ faster, so strong currents are narrow\n[Joy]","x":2410,"y":-30,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g10s7","type":"text","text":"**7 · Gyres and the fast west side**\nCurrents circle the hills: **clockwise in the north, anticlockwise in the south**. The fast flow hugs the **west** of each basin (Gulf Stream, Kuroshio, Agulhas); Stommel (1948) traced this to f changing with latitude (beyond the course). Floating plastic collects at the centre: **garbage patches**. [[Western Boundary Current]]\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g10m5","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• **β** (f varying with latitude) ⇒ west intensification\n• **friction**: mostly at the edges (coasts, sea floor); the open ocean hardly feels it\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":-30,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g10s8","type":"text","text":"**8 · Where the momentum finally goes**\nThe wind keeps adding momentum, so coasts and the sea floor must remove it. At Drake Passage's latitudes no continent blocks the flow: the **Antarctic Circumpolar Current** (≈ **125 ± 11 Sv**) hands its momentum to the sea floor over ridges. **Ledger closed.** Deep water comes up under it; why was it down there? Salt → cascade 11. [[Antarctic Circumpolar Current]]\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g10s9","type":"text","text":"**9 · Take-home: the Arabian Sea cools in mid-summer**\n29.9 °C (May) → 26.7 °C (Aug) despite strong sun: strongest monsoon winds, mixed layer deepens 20 → 62 m, Ekman upwelling off Somalia and Oman plus pumping off the jet's axis, monsoon clouds. The slab budget again: a deeper layer and cold water entering from below. [[Mixed Layer]]\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":190,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g10f1","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/CL24 The Ocean's Ledger III - Wind and the Momentum Ledger.md","x":1390,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g10f2","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Ekman Transport.md","x":1900,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g10f3","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Ekman Pumping.md","x":2410,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g10p1","type":"text","text":"**⚠ Exam traps**\n• Transport is 90° right of the **wind**; the *surface* current is 45° (Nansen's ice 20–40°)\n• Southern Hemisphere: **left**; check which side the coast is before saying up or down\n• Flow goes **around** the hill, not down it\n• Coastal and equatorial upwelling are **not** on the pumping map\n• w = curl(τ/ρf), not curl τ\n📝 Past papers: pointers added in the extras pass.\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280,"color":"6"},
{"id":"g11c","type":"text","text":"**Chain · salt ledger** — evaporation − precipitation sets salinity ⟶ salinity + temperature set density (warm water: T rules, cold water: **salt** rules) ⟶ brine rejection, katabatic winds and polynyas make the densest water ⟶ sinking is rare (few places, mostly winter) ⟶ sunk water keeps its T–S **fingerprint** (NADW, AAIW, AABW) ⟶ the Atlantic exports fresh water to the Pacific, so only the Atlantic forms northern deep water.\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":-930,"width":2020,"height":140},
{"id":"g11s1","type":"text","text":"**1 · E − P writes the salinity**\nSalt stays, only water leaves (evaporation, freezing), so surface salinity (≈ 35 g/kg) records **evaporation − precipitation**. A 50 m layer losing 1 m of water a year gets saltier by 0.7 g/kg yr⁻¹, so currents and mixing must carry the salt away at the same rate. [[Evaporation Minus Precipitation]] [[Seawater Salinity]]\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"2"},
{"id":"g11s2","type":"text","text":"**2 · Salty subtropics, fresh ITCZ and poles**\nSubtropics win twice (clear skies, dry sinking air ⇒ E − P > 0): N Atlantic 37.2 g/kg (+2.8 mm day⁻¹). The ITCZ loses twice (clouds, humid air): E Pacific 33.8 (−3.9). High latitudes: little evaporation, plus rain, rivers and melting ice.\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g11m1","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• Hadley sinking air and ITCZ clouds (the dry and wet belts)\n• cold air holds little vapour (Clausius–Clapeyron)\n• rivers (Amazon, Ganga–Brahmaputra) and melting ice\n• Mediterranean, Red Sea: subtropical, few rivers ⇒ very salty\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g11s3","type":"text","text":"**3 · Why the Atlantic is saltier**\n30°S–60°N surface mean: Atlantic **35.6**, Pacific **34.6**. Trade winds carry **0.32 Sv** (≈ the Amazon) of evaporated Atlantic water across Central America into the Pacific; uncompensated, the Atlantic would gain ≈ 1 g/L per millennium. Keep this: cascade 12 needs it.\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g11m2","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• the narrow **Isthmus of Panama** in the trades' path (the canal plays no part: the transfer is in the air)\n• depends on where the continents are today\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g11s4","type":"text","text":"**4 · Arabian Sea vs Bay of Bengal: a freshwater lid**\nSame latitude and monsoon: **36.2** vs **32.9** g/kg (σ 23.4 vs 20.7). Rivers and rain make a light lid; the wind's stirring stops at its density step, the mixed layer stays shallow and the Bay's surface is warmer (28.3 vs 27.5 °C). Rain, lid and warm surface reinforce each other.\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":-750,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g11m3","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• rivers (Ganga–Brahmaputra, Irrawaddy, Mahanadi) and monsoon rain\n• stratification: a strong density step stops the mixing → cascade 09\n• below ≈ 200 m both seas have the same density (σ ≈ 26.15)\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g11s5","type":"text","text":"**5 · Density: σ and the T–S diagram**\nσ = ρ − 1000 kg m⁻³ (surface water ≈ 27). Potential density removes the pressure effect so waters from different depths can be compared. On a T–S diagram **colder or saltier ⇒ denser**; over the whole ocean T and S matter about equally. [[Potential Density]] [[Water Mass and T-S Diagram]]\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g11s6","type":"text","text":"**6 · Two regimes: warm water by T, cold water by salt**\nPer K of cooling: +0.30 kg m⁻³ at 25 °C but only +0.054 at 0 °C; per g/kg of salt ≈ +0.8 at both. Seawater (S > 24.7) has **no 4 °C maximum**: it keeps getting denser to freezing (−1.92 °C at S = 35), so the coldest water is the densest.\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g11m4","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• T itself: sensitivity to cooling falls ≈ 5.6× from 25 °C to 0 °C (warm: σ lines flat; cold: nearly upright)\n• pressure ⇒ use potential density\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":-30,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g11s7","type":"text","text":"**7 · Brine, katabatic winds, polynyas: the densest water**\nSea ice is almost fresh: freezing rejects **brine**, making cold water saltier and so dense it sinks. Katabatic winds off the Antarctic ice sheet chill the sea and blow new ice offshore, opening polynyas where more ice and brine form. [[Brine Rejection]] [[Katabatic Wind and Polynya]]\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g11m5","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• a **halocline**: a fresh surface layer stays on top, so only that thin layer must reach freezing for ice to form\n• ice blown away from where it formed exports its salt rejection\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":-30,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g11s8","type":"text","text":"**8 · Sinking is rare**\n75 % of the ocean's volume has σ > 27.4 but only ≈ 4 % of surface water (1.4 % in the annual mean). Deep water forms only in winter and in a few places: the Mediterranean and Red Sea, the far North Atlantic (cooling of salty water), patches near Antarctica (brine). Elsewhere: light water on heavy. [[Deep Water Formation]]\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":-280,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g11s9","type":"text","text":"**9 · Water masses: conservative fingerprints**\nT and S are set only at the surface; below it only (weak) mixing changes them, so a parcel keeps its birth fingerprint for centuries. **NADW** 4 °C/35.0 (2–3 km, flows south) · **AAIW** 3.3 °C/34.15 (≈ 1 km, flows north) · **AABW** 0.4 °C/34.67 (bottom, densest, not saltiest). [[North Atlantic Deep Water]] [[Antarctic Intermediate Water]] [[Antarctic Bottom Water]]\n💡 Syrups of different densities in one glass.\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":190,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g11m6","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• mixing two waters of equal σ gives **denser** water (cabbeling): 0 °C/34.0 + 20 °C/38.3 ⇒ +0.56 kg m⁻³\n• core method works with a tracer that does not control density (salinity)\n• oxygen is not conservative (respiration)\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":440,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g11s10","type":"text","text":"**10 · The Pacific forms no deep water**\nThe 165°W section has intermediate and bottom water but **no salty tongue from the north**: the North Pacific is too fresh to sink (the Atlantic exported its fresh water there, step 3). Deep water forms only in the North Atlantic and round Antarctica → cascade 12 (overturning).\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":190,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g11f1","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/CL25 The Ocean's Ledger IV - Salt, Density and Water Masses.md","x":3790,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g11f2","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Water Mass and T-S Diagram.md","x":4300,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g11f3","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Brine Rejection.md","x":4810,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g11p1","type":"text","text":"**⚠ Exam traps**\n• Salinity changes only when water arrives or leaves; evaporation concentrates, rain, rivers and ice melt dilute\n• Warm water: T decides; cold water: salt decides; no 4 °C maximum when S > 24.7\n• AABW is denser than NADW although **less** salty (it is colder)\n• NADW forms by cooling salty water, AABW by brine rejection\n• mixing two waters of equal σ gives denser water\n📝 Past papers: pointers added in the extras pass.\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":680,"width":480,"height":280,"color":"6"},
{"id":"g12c","type":"text","text":"**Chain · mass ledger** — heavy water sinks in only two places (N Atlantic, Antarctica) ⟶ not the N Pacific (too fresh) ⟶ the same mass rises mostly in the wind-pulled Southern Ocean ⟶ ~1,000-year round trip ⟶ RAPID watches it ⟶ salt carried north keeps the north heavy (positive feedback) ⟶ **two stable states + hysteresis** ⟶ AR6: very likely to weaken, collapse not expected before 2100.\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":1270,"width":2020,"height":140},
{"id":"g12s1","type":"text","text":"**1 · The mass ledger: where water sinks**\nHeavy water sinks, sea level does not fall, so the same mass must rise elsewhere. Deep water forms only in the **North Atlantic** (15–20 Sv; Nordic and Labrador Seas, cooling of salty water) and **round Antarctica** (≈ 10 Sv; Weddell, Ross: brine). Gulf Stream ≈ 30 Sv, ACC ≈ 125 Sv. [[Deep Water Formation]]\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"2"},
{"id":"g12s2","type":"text","text":"**2 · Why not the North Pacific?**\nSurface salinity 32.8 vs 35.1 g/kg in the N Atlantic. Cooled to −1.8 °C it reaches only σ = 26.4, lighter than the 27.7 water at 2,000 m beneath: it would need S ≈ 34.4 (1.6 g/kg more). The Atlantic exports its fresh water to the Pacific (0.32 Sv, cascade 11).\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g12m1","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• temperature is *not* the difference: both oceans cool to freezing\n• the trades' fresh-water export across Central America\n• oxygen agrees: deep N Atlantic is oxygen-rich, the N Pacific shows no downwelling\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":1700,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g12s3","type":"text","text":"**3 · Coming back up: the Southern Ocean**\nBelow the mixed layer water moves along density surfaces; south of ≈ 45°S those surfaces (σ 27.2–27.8) tilt up to the surface. The westerlies drive Ekman transport to the left (north), so water is pulled up ≈ 25 m yr⁻¹ at 50–62°S: NADW rises here. [[Antarctic Circumpolar Current]]\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g12m2","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• the westerlies (cascade 10: same pumping)\n• eddies, which partly cancel the wind-driven overturning\n• part of the risen water re-sinks as bottom water, part goes north as intermediate water\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":1700,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g12s4","type":"text","text":"**4 · The slow loop: ~1,000 years**\nWater is replaced only as fast as it sinks and returns: turnover ≈ 1,000 yr, a mean deep flow of ≈ 1 mm s⁻¹ (≈ 31,600 km of route). Oldest water is at mid-depth in the North Pacific, not the bottom. The Southern Ocean warms slowly because its upwelled water has not seen the surface for centuries.\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g12m3","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• 1 mm s⁻¹ is hidden by 10 cm s⁻¹ deep currents, so we use tracers (T–S, oxygen, tritium, CFCs)\n• oxygen saturation: deep N Pacific 10–15 %, N Atlantic ≈ 85 %\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":1700,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g12s5","type":"text","text":"**5 · Measuring it: RAPID**\nMoorings across the Atlantic at 26.5°N since 2004: mean **17.0 Sv**, 10-day values −4 to 32 Sv, yearly means 14.9–19.1 Sv. Twenty years against a 1,000-year memory cannot show a trend yet; it shows how the overturning works and gives a baseline.\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g12s6","type":"text","text":"**6 · The salt feedback (Stommel's two boxes)**\nThe flow carries salty water north, which keeps the north heavy, which keeps it sinking. Fresh water in the north makes it lighter, the flow weakens, less salt arrives, the north gets fresher still: a **positive feedback**, like ice–albedo. [[Stommel Two-Box Model]]\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"1"},
{"id":"g12m4","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• fresh water: high-latitude rain, rivers, Greenland melt\n• a cooler north is denser: a counter-feedback that stabilises the 'on' state\n[Joy]","x":4810,"y":2170,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g12s7","type":"text","text":"**7 · Two stable states and hysteresis**\nJoy's toy: salt carried = y|1−y| against forcing E; the 'on' state ceases to exist beyond a fold (E = ¼), so the flow weakens gradually, then **jumps** to the reversed (off) state, and stays off when the fresh water is removed. Same fresh water, two states: where you are depends on how you got there. [[Hysteresis]]\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g12m5","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• the toy fixes temperatures and omits the Southern Ocean winds; how close the real Atlantic is to the fold is unknown\n• it has happened: Greenland warmed ≈ 8 °C in 40 yr ~11,500 yr ago; Heinrich events\n[Joy]","x":4300,"y":2170,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g12s8","type":"text","text":"**8 · Could it switch off? (IPCC AR6)**\nThe AMOC is **very likely to weaken** in the 21st century in all scenarios (high confidence in the decline, low confidence in its size); **medium confidence of no abrupt collapse before 2100**. A collapse would shift the tropical rain belt south and weaken the African and Asian monsoons. [[Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation]]\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g12s9","type":"text","text":"**9 · Why the AMOC matters beyond the ocean**\nUnlike the Hadley cells, the AMOC carries heat from one hemisphere to the other: the N Atlantic loses ≈ 0.9 PW. This is why the ITCZ sits north of the equator on average: it lies on the warmer side, so the air carries part of that heat back across. → cascades 06 and 08.\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":2390,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g12m6","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• weaker AMOC ⇒ cooler north ⇒ ITCZ shifts south ⇒ Indian monsoon weakens ('the Asian monsoon is ours')\n[Joy]","x":3790,"y":2640,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g12f1","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/CL26 The Ocean's Ledger V - Mass, the Overturning and the Switch.md","x":3790,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g12f2","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation.md","x":4300,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g12f3","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Stommel Two-Box Model.md","x":4810,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g12p1","type":"text","text":"**⚠ Exam traps**\n• Use **Joy's** Stommel version (fixed temperatures, fold at E = ¼), not the interactive tool's model\n• Both oceans get cold enough: the Pacific is too **fresh**, not too warm\n• Oldest water is mid-depth in the N Pacific, not at the bottom\n• 20 years of RAPID is not a trend; a 0.9 Sv per decade slope is inside the noise\n• 'very likely to weaken' is not 'collapse'\n📝 Past papers: pointers added in the extras pass.\n[Joy]","x":5320,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280,"color":"6"},
{"id":"g13c","type":"text","text":"**Chain · ENSO** — trades + equatorial upwelling ⟶ cold tongue, warm pool, tilted thermocline ⟶ trades weaken ⟶ **Bjerknes feedback** (positive) warms the east ⟶ Kelvin wave east, Rossby waves west and back ⟶ delayed negative feedback turns it round ⟶ an oscillation of 2–7 years, not a clock ⟶ ocean memory makes it forecastable ⟶ the whole planet feels it.\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":1270,"width":2020,"height":140},
{"id":"g13s1","type":"text","text":"**1 · Trades and equatorial upwelling**\nThe trades blow from the east. Ekman transport is to the right north of the equator and to the left south of it, so water leaves the equator on both sides and cold water rises from below: the warm surface layer is thin and easy to change. [[Walker Circulation]]\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"2"},
{"id":"g13s2","type":"text","text":"**2 · The tilted thermocline**\nOn the equator f = 0, so the wind's westward push cannot be balanced by Coriolis: a **pressure gradient** balances it. Water piles up in the west; the thermocline is deep in the west, shallow in the east. East = **cold tongue**, west = **warm pool** under the rain. [[Thermocline and Pycnocline]]\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g13m1","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• the strength of the easterly stress and the **width** of the basin (nothing interrupts the Pacific trades)\n• a 5 K change cannot come from sunlight; only moving warm water and the thermocline can supply it\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":1700,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g13s3","type":"text","text":"**3 · The trades weaken**\nA westerly wind burst, a wave or chance weakens the trades. The push that held the water in the west is gone but the **pressure gradient it built remains**, now unbalanced: over weeks to months the warm layer relaxes toward flat.\n[Joy]","x":2410,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"2"},
{"id":"g13m2","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• a steeply tilted thermocline (La Niña state) stores potential energy, so an instability can occur\n• normal state is locked: strong trades keep the rain west, the rising air reinforces the trades\n[Joy]","x":2410,"y":1700,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g13s4","type":"text","text":"**4 · The Bjerknes feedback**\nWeaker trades ⇒ thermocline flattens, less cold water comes up ⇒ the east warms ⇒ the east–west SST contrast shrinks and the rain moves east ⇒ the trades weaken further. Two ledgers in one loop: winds = momentum, temperatures = energy. [[Bjerknes Feedback]]\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":1450,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"1"},
{"id":"g13m3","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• coupling: the rain follows the warm water, so atmosphere and ocean push each other\n• the same kind of positive feedback as Stommel's salt feedback (cascade 12)\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":1700,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g13s5","type":"text","text":"**5 · El Niño: the east is warm**\nThe east warms by 2–4 K a few months after the wave. 8–14 Nov 2026: +3.1 °C in the Niño-3.4 box, +5.4 °C off Peru (anomaly = deviation from the 1991–2020 mean). 5 K with no change in sunlight is huge. [[ENSO]] [[Climate Anomaly]]\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g13s6","type":"text","text":"**6 · Why it turns round: Kelvin and Rossby waves**\nThe wind change sends a **Kelvin wave east** (deepens the warm layer in the east) and **Rossby waves west**. These reflect off the western boundary and return with the **opposite** effect: they thin the eastern warm layer and cool the east, about six months after the warming began.\n[Joy]","x":2410,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g13m4","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• reflection at the western boundary\n• recharge: a full cycle discharges warm water, restored only slowly by the trades\n• coastal Kelvin waves carry warmth poleward along the coast\n[Joy]","x":2410,"y":2170,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g13s7","type":"text","text":"**7 · An oscillation, not a clock**\nA fast loop that feeds itself (Bjerknes) plus a slow, delayed loop that undoes it (waves) = the **delayed oscillator**. Period 2–7 yr, mean ≈ 4 yr. A positive feedback with an *instantaneous* negative one gives two states and a switch; with a *delayed* one, an oscillation. [[Delayed Oscillator]]\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"5"},
{"id":"g13m5","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• noise: the record is messier than a sine\n• other basins do it too: the Indian Ocean Dipole and the Atlantic Niño\n[Joy]","x":1900,"y":2170,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g13s8","type":"text","text":"**8 · Memory makes it forecastable**\nτ = C/λ: the air forgets in about a week (weather is not forecastable beyond ~2 weeks) but the top 50 m of sea remembers for over a year, so a November El Niño forecast reaches March 2027 (NOAA CPC, 12 Nov). The same memory is what commits us to warming (cascade 09). [[Low-Pass Filter]]\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":1920,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g13s9","type":"text","text":"**9 · The whole planet feels it**\nGlobal mean air temperature spikes with strong El Niños (1997–98, 2015–16, 2023) on top of the warming trend. Moving the rain moves the atmosphere's engine (latent heat): teleconnections to the Amazon, North America, Europe and India (El Niño years tend to be dry, but not always). → cascade 08.\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":2390,"width":480,"height":230,"color":"4"},
{"id":"g13m6","type":"text","text":"**⚙ Affected by**\n• the Indian Ocean Dipole: one reason the India link is not fixed\n• the tropical ocean is the atmosphere's engine, so shifting the rain shifts global weather\n[Joy]","x":1390,"y":2640,"width":480,"height":190,"color":"3"},
{"id":"g13f1","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/CL29 The Ocean's Ledger VI - Slow Swings and the Ocean's Memory for Carbon.md","x":1390,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g13f2","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Bjerknes Feedback.md","x":1900,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g13f3","type":"file","file":"01-Climate/Concepts/Delayed Oscillator.md","x":2410,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280},
{"id":"g13p1","type":"text","text":"**⚠ Exam traps**\n• Bjerknes alone is a positive feedback: it needs the **delayed** wave feedback to come back\n• On the equator the wind is balanced by a pressure gradient, not Coriolis\n• Kelvin wave goes **east**, Rossby waves **west**, and the reflection reverses the sign\n• ENSO is not periodic: 2–7 yr, not a clock\n• an anomaly is relative to a stated mean\n📝 Past papers: pointers added in the extras pass.\n[Joy]","x":2920,"y":2880,"width":480,"height":280,"color":"6"},
{"id":"legend","type":"text","x":-1010,"y":650,"width":2020,"height":300,"text":"**How to read this map**\n**Hub (here):** one bubble per cascade, placed exactly where its group sits around the hub. Arrows between bubbles = what causes / feeds what across topics.\n**Each group = one cascade:** chain summary → numbered steps (follow the arrows) → ⚙ modifiers → 🔗 note cards → ⚠ exam traps / 📝 past papers.\n**Colours:** orange = trigger · cyan = step · green = outcome · red = feedback loop · yellow = modifier · purple = exam trap / past paper.  **Tags:** [Argha] [S-pre] [S-post] [Joy] = who taught it.  **💡 = intuition / analogy** (to help you picture it, not course text)."}
],
